{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-49247",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{2}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)+7={\\int }_{2}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 3={\\int }_{2}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{8}(5f(x)+4)\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{2}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+4{\\int }_{2}^{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5(3)+4\\cdot x\\,{|}_{2}^{8} \\\\  & =15+4(8-2) \\\\  & =39.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
